  "slides": [
    {
      "page_title": "What a Bash Function Really Is",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Definition",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Named block of commands held in shell memory",
            "Runs in the *current* shell process — no new process",
            "Defining is a runtime act, not a compile-time one",
            "A `def` inside an `if` exists only after that branch runs",
            "Redefine freely: last definition wins, no warning"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Two Syntaxes"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "greet() { echo hi; }        # POSIX, portable",
            "function greet { echo hi; } # Bash keyword",
            "function greet() { :; }     # mixed: Bash only"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Prefer the POSIX form — dash and `sh` accept it",
            "Mixed form is legal in Bash, a parse error in dash",
            "Body must be a compound command",
            "`{` needs a space after it, `}` needs `;` or newline before"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Parens Make a Subshell"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "work() ( cd /tmp; make )  # body IS a subshell",
            "work                       # cd never escapes"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Lookup Order"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "alias (parse time)",
              "function",
              "builtin",
              "PATH exec"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "ls() { command ls --color \"$@\"; }",
            "# `command` skips the function,",
            "# else ls calls itself forever"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Inspect and Remove"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Command",
            "Does"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`declare -f f`",
              "print body"
            ],
            [
              "`declare -F`",
              "names only"
            ],
            [
              "`unset -f f`",
              "drop function"
            ],
            [
              "`unset f`",
              "hits variable first"
            ],
            [
              "`type -t f`",
              "function/builtin/alias/file/keyword"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Exporting",
          "items": [
            "Children do NOT inherit functions by default",
            "`export -f f` puts it in the environment",
            "Env key looks like `BASH_FUNC_f%%`"
          ],
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "cards",
          "spec": {
            "cards": [
              {
                "title": "Function",
                "lines": [
                  "zero fork",
                  "same shell state",
                  "sees your vars",
                  "can change them"
                ]
              },
              {
                "title": "Script",
                "lines": [
                  "fork + exec",
                  "own process",
                  "own env copy",
                  "changes die"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Core idea",
          "text": "A function is code sharing your shell's memory. That is its whole power and its whole danger."
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "main() { setup; run \"$@\"; }",
            "main \"$@\"     # last line of the file"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Golden rule",
          "text": "Always write `\"$@\"` with quotes. Bare `$@` word-splits and glob-expands every argument you were handed."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Arguments: How They Arrive",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Positional Parameters",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Args arrive as `$1 $2 ...`, exactly like a script's",
            "They SHADOW the script's own `$1 $2` during the call",
            "`$0` is the exception — still the script name, not the function",
            "`$#` = count of args passed to the function",
            "`FUNCNAME[0]` is the function's name; `FUNCNAME` is the stack"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "array",
          "spec": {
            "cells": [
              "$0 script",
              "$1 Bob",
              "$2 42",
              "$# 2",
              "$@ Bob 42"
            ],
            "indices": true,
            "pointers": [],
            "highlight": [],
            "dim": [],
            "caption": "greet Bob 42"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "`$@` versus `$*`"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Form",
            "Expands to"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`\"$@\"`",
              "N quoted words"
            ],
            [
              "`\"$*\"`",
              "one word, joined by `IFS[0]`"
            ],
            [
              "`$@` bare",
              "split + globbed"
            ],
            [
              "`\"${@:2}\"`",
              "args from 2 on"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Defaults and Guards"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "local name=\"${1:-anon}\"   # unset OR empty",
            "local nick=\"${1-anon}\"    # unset only",
            "local f=\"${1:?path required}\"  # abort+msg"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Consuming Args"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "shift        # drop $1, renumber rest",
            "shift 2      # drop two",
            "shift        # past $# -> non-zero status",
            "set -- a b c # rewrite MY params only"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`set --` inside a function never touches the caller's params",
            "`${10}` needs braces — `$10` parses as `$1` then `0`",
            "`f \"${arr[@]}\"` flattens to N args; no true array parameter"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Passing a Name, Not a Value"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "read_cfg() { local -n out=$1; out=loaded; }",
            "read_cfg result   # Bash 4.3+ nameref",
            "echo \"$result\"     # loaded"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Why did `f my file.txt` see two arguments?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "Quoting must happen at the CALL site: `f \"$name\"`",
            "Words already split cannot be rejoined inside",
            "Calls take no parens and no commas: `greet Bob 42`"
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "greet(Bob, 42)   # wrong: one literal arg"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "`return N`",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Sets the function's exit status, 0–255, taken modulo 256",
            "`return 300` lands as 44; `return -1` lands as 255",
            "Bare `return` reuses the LAST command's status",
            "Falling off the end does exactly the same thing",
            "0 = success — inverted from C and Python truthiness"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Reading `$?`"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "fetch_cfg; rc=$?    # capture on the NEXT line",
            "echo done           # this would overwrite $?",
            "(( rc == 2 )) && die 'bad input'"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Exit Status: Codes, Predicates, stdout",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Standard Codes"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Code",
            "Meaning"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "1",
              "generic error"
            ],
            [
              "2",
              "misuse of builtin"
            ],
            [
              "126",
              "not executable"
            ],
            [
              "127",
              "command not found"
            ],
            [
              "128+N",
              "killed by signal N"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Predicates"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "is_root() { [[ $EUID -eq 0 ]]; }  # no return",
            "if is_root; then setup; fi",
            "is_root && harden",
            "if ! validate \"$f\"; then skip; fi   # ! inverts"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "The test's own status becomes the function's status",
            "Usable directly in `if`, `while`, `&&`, `||`",
            "`return` outside a function or sourced file is an error"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The `local` Status Eater"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "local x=$(cmd)        # status is local's: 0",
            "local x; x=$(cmd) || return 1   # correct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Why",
          "text": "`local` is itself a command and always succeeds, so its 0 replaces whatever `cmd` reported."
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "cards",
          "spec": {
            "cards": [
              {
                "title": "`return`",
                "lines": [
                  "leaves function",
                  "caller continues",
                  "library-safe"
                ]
              },
              {
                "title": "`exit`",
                "lines": [
                  "kills whole shell",
                  "caller never resumes",
                  "accidental script-killer"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Codes as vocabulary",
          "items": [
            "0 found, 1 not found, 2 bad input",
            "Document each one above the function",
            "Prefer small explicit codes over `return -1`"
          ],
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Data Leaves via stdout",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A function 'returns' data by WRITING it to stdout",
            "Caller captures with `result=$(f)`",
            "Status and data are two independent channels",
            "`$(...)` strips ALL trailing newlines from the capture"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "out=$(fetch_name); status=$?  # both channels",
            "(( status == 0 )) || return 1",
            "printf '%s\\n' \"$out\""
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "printf 'warn: %s\\n' \"$msg\" >&2  # human",
            "printf '%s\\n' \"$value\"          # data",
            "f >/dev/null   # keep status, drop data"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Output: The Data Channel",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Capture Forks"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "caller",
              "fork subshell",
              "f runs",
              "stdout back",
              "vars lost"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Keep Channels Apart"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Diagnostics go to stderr, never stdout",
            "Mixing logs with results = 'my variable has the log line in it'",
            "`echo` mangles a leading `-` and eats backslashes",
            "Use `printf '%s\\n' \"$x\"` for anything you mean literally"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Many Values"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "list_users() { printf '%s\\n' ann bob; }",
            "mapfile -t arr < <(list_users)  # parent reads",
            "list_users | mapfile -t arr     # array lost"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "One item per line is the shell's list format",
            "Process substitution keeps the reader in your shell",
            "A pipe puts `mapfile` in a subshell — array vanishes"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Fork-Free Returns"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "f() { local -n r=$1; r=value; }",
            "f out; echo \"$out\"      # no fork",
            "g() { REPLY=value; }    # read/select style"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Way",
            "Wins",
            "Costs"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`$(f)`",
              "clean, composable",
              "a fork, state lost"
            ],
            [
              "nameref",
              "fast, stateful",
              "harder to nest"
            ],
            [
              "`REPLY`",
              "no fork",
              "not reentrant"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Counter always 0?",
          "text": "`f | while read ...` runs the `while` in a subshell under Bash defaults. The count is real — it just dies with the fork."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Dynamic, Not Lexical",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A callee SEES the caller's locals — and can modify them",
            "Scope follows the call stack, not the source text",
            "`local x` covers the function and everything it calls",
            "The previous value is restored when the function returns"
          ]
        },
        {
