        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Declaring"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "local a b c            # three locals",
            "local a=1 b=2          # each = is one word",
            "local msg=\"hello there\"  # so quote values"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Local Variables and Scope",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Flag",
            "Makes"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`local -r x=5`",
              "read-only for the call"
            ],
            [
              "`local -i n`",
              "integer"
            ],
            [
              "`local -a arr`",
              "indexed array"
            ],
            [
              "`local -A map`",
              "associative array"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Without `local`"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Every assignment is GLOBAL — silently clobbers the caller",
            "Loop counters `i`, `n`, `f`, `tmp` are the usual victims",
            "Nested loops in caller and callee overwrite each other",
            "`unset x` inside can unset the CALLER's variable"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Nameref Collision"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "f() { local -n out=$1; out=hi; }",
            "out=x; f out   # circular name reference",
            "g() { local -n _ref=$1; _ref=hi; }  # safe"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Why does `local` fail at the top of my script?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "It is only valid inside a function body",
            "Error: 'local: can only be used in a function'",
            "`declare` inside a function acts like `local`",
            "`declare` at top level is global — the subtle half"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "The #1 `local` gotcha",
          "text": "`local x=$(cmd)` masks the exit status. Split the declaration from the assignment whenever failure matters."
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Discipline",
          "items": [
            "Declare every variable at the top with `local`",
            "Treat globals as read-only unless documented",
            "`set -u` turns unset reads into hard errors"
          ],
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Definition",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A forked copy of the shell process",
            "Inherits all state; nothing it changes flows back",
            "Every fork is a real `fork()` syscall"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "cards",
          "spec": {
