    {
      "page_title": "Subshells: What They Lose",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Classic Loss"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "n=0",
            "cat f | while read l; do n=$((n+1)); done",
            "echo $n   # 0 — while ran in a subshell"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Fix 1: `while read l; do ...; done < f`",
            "Fix 2: `done < <(cmd)` — process substitution",
            "Fix 3: `shopt -s lastpipe`, job control off, Bash only"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Use One on Purpose"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "( cd /tmp && do_work )   # cd cannot escape",
            "# no need to cd - afterwards"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Silent surprises",
          "items": [
            "`exit` in a subshell exits only that subshell",
            "You meant to abort; you aborted nothing",
            "`trap` handlers reset there (ignored signals aside)",
            "Parent `EXIT` traps do not fire inside"
          ],
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Cost",
          "text": "Thousands of forks dominate runtime in a hot loop. Hoist `$( )` out of the loop, or write into a nameref instead."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "`set -e` Is Conditional",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "errexit is suppressed for any command in a condition",
            "That means `if f`, `f && g`, `! f`",
            "So a function called under `if` runs with errexit off",
            "Off for its WHOLE body, including nested calls"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Consequence",
          "text": "`if deploy; then` quietly disables every safety net inside `deploy`. Write explicit `|| return 1` instead of trusting `set -e`."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Pipelines"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "set -o pipefail   # first non-zero wins",
            "gen | filter | sink",
            "echo \"${PIPESTATUS[0]}\"  # gen's own status"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Strict Header"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "set -euo pipefail",
            "IFS=$'\\n\\t'   # no space-splitting",
            "# know each flag before adopting this"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Traps"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Trap",
            "Fires",
            "Needs"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`EXIT`",
              "shell exits",
              "—"
            ],
            [
              "`RETURN`",
              "function returns",
              "`set -T`"
            ],
            [
              "`ERR`",
              "command fails",
              "`set -E` to inherit"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Without `-E`, `ERR` traps skip functions and `$( )`",
            "`set -T` (functrace) does the same for `RETURN` and `DEBUG`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "tmp=$(mktemp -d)",
            "trap 'rm -rf \"$tmp\"' EXIT   # set it NOW",
            "# no path can leak after this line"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Errors, Traps and Strict Mode",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Failing Loudly"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "die() { printf '%s\\n' \"$*\" >&2; exit 1; }",
            "printf 'err %s:%s\\n' \"${BASH_SOURCE[0]}\" \"$LINENO\"",
            "caller 0   # one stack frame; caller 1 for more"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Two edge cases",
          "items": [
            "`set -e` inside `$( )` binds the subshell only",
            "Its failure matters only if you check the status",
            "`local x=$(fail)` never triggers errexit — `local` ate it"
          ],
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Recursion",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Functions may recurse; depth is unlimited by default",
            "`FUNCNEST=100` caps it and catches runaway recursion",
            "Every working variable must be `local`",
            "Dynamic scope gives no per-frame isolation otherwise"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "fact() {",
            "  local n=$1                 # per-frame",
            "  ((n<=1)) && { echo 1; return; }",
            "  echo $(( n * $(fact $((n-1))) ))",
            "}"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Cost",
          "text": "Each `$( )` level forks once — depth 20 means 20 forks. Accumulate into a global or nameref to stay fork-free."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Live Call Stack"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "matrix",
          "spec": {
            "rows": [
              [
                "[0] fact",
                "[1] compute"
              ],
              [
                "[2] main",
                "[3] source"
              ]
            ],
            "highlight": [],
            "caption": "FUNCNAME"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`FUNCNAME[1]` is the caller's name",
            "`BASH_LINENO` and `BASH_SOURCE` are parallel arrays",
            "Index all three together to print a traceback"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Dispatch by Name"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "run() { \"$1\" \"${@:2}\"; }   # higher order",
            "case $cmd in",
            "  start|stop|status) \"cmd_$cmd\" \"$@\" ;;",
            "  *) usage ;;",
            "esac"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Validate the name BEFORE calling it",
            "Never `eval \"$user_input\"` to dispatch",
            "Whitelist with `case` or an associative array"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Nesting and Sourcing"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A function defined inside another becomes GLOBAL once the outer runs",
            "`source lib.sh` loads functions into the current shell",
            "That is Bash's whole import system — nothing more"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "[[ ${BASH_SOURCE[0]} != \"$0\" ]] ||",
            "  { echo 'source me'; exit 1; }"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Composing",
          "text": "`list_files | filter_big | count` works fine — but every stage is a subshell, so no stage can set a caller variable."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Recursion, Composition, Call Mechanics",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Crossing processes",
          "items": [
            "`export -f f` then `bash -c 'f'` works",
            "`find -exec` needs `bash -c` for the same reason",
            "Exec'd binaries cannot see shell functions"
          ],
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Quoting and Capture",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Unquoted `$@` splits on whitespace and globs — write `\"$@\"`",
            "`local x=$(cmd)` hides failure — split the two statements",
            "`echo` for data mangles `-n` and backslashes — use `printf '%s\\n'`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "local x; x=$(cmd) || return 1  # status kept",
            "printf '%s\\n' \"$x\"             # literal out"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Wrong Channel"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`return \"$str\"` gives 'numeric argument required' — print it instead",
            "`exit` in a library function kills the caller's script",
            "Rule: `return` in libraries, `exit` only inside `main`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Lost to a Subshell"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "cmd | while read l; do ((n++)); done  # n=0",
            "while read l; do ((n++)); done < <(cmd)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Scope and Timing"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Missing `local` clobbers the caller's `i`, `f`, `n`, `tmp`",
            "Reading `$?` late — an `echo` or `[[ ]]` already overwrote it",
            "Capture `local rc=$?` on the very next line"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Syntax and Order"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Symptom",
            "Cause"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "'unexpected token'",
              "no space in `{ cmd; }`"
            ],
            [
              "'command not found'",
              "called above its definition"
            ],
            [
              "captured value short",
              "`$( )` ate trailing newlines"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "out=$(printf 'a\\n\\n'; printf x)  # sentinel",
            "out=${out%x}      # newlines preserved"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Define functions at the top, or wrap in `main` called last",
            "A straight-line script runs definitions in order — nothing hoists"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Infinite recursion",
          "text": "Naming a function after a builtin or PATH command makes it call itself. Reach the real one with `command ls` or `builtin cd`."
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Two false hopes",
          "items": [
            "`set -e` does not abort inside a function under `if`",
            "Fix it with explicit `|| return`",
            "`export -f` is Bash-to-Bash only — python children see nothing"
          ],
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Write the Contract"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "# args: $1 path, $2 mode (default ro)",
            "# stdout: parsed value, one per line",
            "# exit: 0 ok, 1 missing, 2 bad input",
            "parse_config() { ...; }"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Common Mistakes and Their Fixes",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Naming",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "One function, one job",
            "Name it `verb_noun`: `fetch_config`, `validate_input`",
            "Lowercase with underscores, always",
            "Prefix library functions: `mylib::parse` or `mylib_parse`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Keep functions under ~50 lines",
            "If a section needs a comment, it is another function",
            "Prefer `\"$@\"` pass-through over rebuilding arg lists"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "One Channel Each"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Channel",
            "Carries"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "exit status",
              "success / failure"
            ],
            [
              "stdout",
              "data for the caller"
            ],
            [
              "stderr",
              "messages for humans"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Skeleton to Memorise"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "f() {",
            "  local a=\"${1:?}\" b=\"${2:-default}\"",
            "  printf '%s\\n' \"$out\"",
            "  return 0",
            "}"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Speed and Isolation"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Avoid subshells in hot loops; hoist `$( )` out",
            "Use namerefs or globals when performance matters",
            "Use `( subshell )` when you WANT `cd`, `set` or `trap` contained",
            "Test by sourcing the library and calling functions directly",
            "Keep side effects out of the definition-time path"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Run `shellcheck`",
          "items": [
            "Catches unquoted `$@`",
            "Catches `local x=$(...)` status loss",
            "Catches `while read` subshell bugs"
          ],
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Feature",
            "Portable?"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`local`",
              "not POSIX, universal"
            ],
            [
              "`function` keyword",
              "Bash only"
            ],
            [
              "arrays, `[[ ]]`",
              "Bash only"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "cards",
          "spec": {
            "cards": [
              {
                "title": "Same shell",
                "lines": [
                  "function call",
                  "`source lib.sh`",
                  "`{ ...; }`"
                ]
              },
              {
                "title": "State lost",
                "lines": [
                  "`( )` and `$( )`",
                  "each pipe stage",
                  "a separate script"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Know the ceiling",
          "text": "A Bash function returns bytes and one small integer — nothing else. Data structures, floats or error objects mean it is time for a real language."
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "run_backup",
            "rc=$?        # the very next line",
            "(( rc == 0 )) || die 'backup'"
          ],
          "note": "copy it before anything else runs"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Status Capture and Safe Cleanup",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. What $? Holds",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`$?` = status of the ==most recent foreground command==",
            "One slot only — it is **overwritten**, never stacked",
            "A function call sets it from its `return` or last command",
            "Background jobs use `$!` and `wait`, not `$?`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Read It Immediately"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`cmd; echo ok; [[ $? -eq 0 ]]` tests `echo`",
            "A log line between call and test destroys the status",
            "`[[ ]]`, `(( ))` and `printf` all reset it too"
          ],
          "style": "cross"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "deploy",
            "log 'deploy done'   # sets $? = 0",
            "echo $?             # log's status!"
          ],
          "note": "any command overwrites $?"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "After this",
            "`$?` is"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`f`",
              "f's status"
            ],
            [
              "`f | g`",
              "g's status"
            ],
            [
              "`! f`",
              "inverted"
            ],
            [
              "`rc=$?`",
              "0, from the assign"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Capture, then act",
          "text": "`$?` survives exactly __one__ command. Copy it into `rc=$?` on the line right after the call, then branch on `$rc` as often as you like."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Temp Dir Cleanup"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "tmp=$(mktemp -d) || exit 1",
            "trap 'rm -rf \"$tmp\"' EXIT",
            "# work freely from here"
          ],
          "note": "trap on the line right after mktemp"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`mktemp -d` makes a unique, private directory",
            "Set the trap ==immediately==, nothing in between",
            "Single quotes: `$tmp` expands when the trap **fires**",
            "`EXIT` covers return, error, `set -e` and Ctrl-C exits"
          ],
          "style": "check"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Why the Gap Leaks"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "mktemp -d",
              "trap ... EXIT",
              "real work",
              "exit anywhere",
              "dir removed"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Where cleanup fails",
          "items": [
            "Any command before the trap can abort and leak",
            "Unquoted `rm -rf $tmp` splits on spaces",
            "Empty `$tmp` turns it into `rm -rf /`",
            "A second `trap ... EXIT` replaces the first"
          ],
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "One line apart",
          "text": "`mktemp` then `trap` with **nothing** between them is the whole pattern. Every command you slip into that gap is a path that can leak."
        }
      ],
      "subtitle": "Two habits, one line each"
    }
  ],
